---
// The page-level attribution surface. Markup, styles and one asset import; no
// logic — nothing here has a type or a branch, which is what the no-logic rule
// in AGENTS.md counts. See docs/spec/attribution-surface.md.

// Inlined `?raw`, as the header's own mark is: one copy of the artwork, in
// src/assets/, rather than a second pasted into this template to drift. A 16px
// glyph is not worth a request, and `currentColor` only reaches an inline SVG.
import githubMarkSvg from '../assets/github-mark.svg?raw';
---

<!--
  Bottom-left, and it stays there at every viewport.

  The page has four corners and three are spoken for: the header owns top-left,
  the desktop card owns bottom-right, and on a phone the full-bleed sheet owns
  the whole bottom edge whenever a card is open. Measured rather than argued —
  top-right lands 3211px² on the header caption at 375px, and bottom-left is
  100% behind the sheet on both phone viewports with a card open.

  ⚠️ The sheet occlusion is **accepted, not fixed**: occluded by a dismissible,
  non-modal overlay is not the same as conditionally rendered — the element is in
  the DOM and displayed on load, and the sheet is one gesture from dismissal. The
  alternative (re-homing into the header lockup below the breakpoint) is clear
  everywhere and costs a DOM re-home rather than a media query, which would give
  `(max-width: 700px), (max-height: 500px)` a **third** holder.
-->
<div class="pattr">
  <!--
    Google's own `poweredby.png`, vendored **unaltered** and served from this
    origin. Not hotlinked: this page makes zero third-party requests, and a
    hotlinked PNG would disclose every visitor's IP and referer to Google on a
    `noindex` site, to satisfy an obligation that has nothing to do with privacy.

    ⚠️ **62x30, measured** — the spec assumed 144x26, which was a guess nobody
    had checked. The `width`/`height` attributes below stay at those numbers
    because they describe the *file*; the CSS draws it at half that. See
    [ADR-0048](../../../../docs/adr/0048-google-attribution-is-a-vendored-page-element.md)
    for what half costs on a 1x display.

    ⚠️ **It is dark on transparent** — 554 of its 613 opaque pixels — so on a
    page whose background is #1a1613 it is invisible, and Google publishes no
    light variant (`poweredby_lt.png` and its siblings all 404). Hence the plate
    underneath: the *image* is displayed exactly as Google serves it, which is
    what "do not change any of the Google marks" asks, and the surface it sits on
    is ours to choose.
  -->
  <!--
    ⚠️ **Not `loading="lazy"`**, which it was for about ten minutes. A lazy image
    loads when the browser decides it is needed, and this one's entire premise is
    that it is *always displayed* — deferring it is a smaller version of the
    hotlinking failure #104 rejected, where the obligation is met by something
    outside our control. It is 3.4 KB.
  -->
  <img class="pattr-mark" src="/poweredby-google.png" alt="powered by Google" width="62" height="30" />
  <!--
    The first page chrome on this site that is not inert. The surface sets itself
    `pointer-events: none` so drag-to-look-around passes straight through it; the
    anchor has to opt back out.
  -->
  <a class="pattr-link" href="/attribution">Attribution</a>
  <!--
    The source, in the same row rather than a corner of its own: the other three
    are spoken for, as the comment at the top says. A plain navigation, not a
    request — the page still makes zero third-party requests until it is clicked.
  -->
  <!--
    The mark is decorative — the text already says GitHub — so the SVG carries
    aria-hidden itself and a screen reader reads the link once.
  -->
  <a class="pattr-link pattr-source" href="https://github.com/mephistopheles4/stacks"
    ><span class="pattr-icon" set:html={githubMarkSvg} />View on GitHub</a
  >
</div>

<style>
  .pattr {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    left: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    /* Above the shelf, below the card and the debug surfaces. */
    z-index: 5;
    /* Inert by default — the shelf is dragged through this corner. */
    pointer-events: none;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: #6f6051;
  }

  /*
    The plate, and the reason it exists is in the markup above: the image is dark
    on transparent and this page is near-black. Deliberately the *quietest* light
    that keeps it legible rather than a white chip — Google's own constraint is
    that the graphic must not be the most prominent element on the page, and on a
    dark room with one lit bookcase a bright rectangle in the corner would be
    exactly that.
  */
  .pattr-mark {
    display: block;
    /*
      Half the file's 62x30, and every other number here halved with it so the
      plate stays the same shape rather than becoming a border.

      ⚠️ **Scaled, never cropped or recoloured** — the whole graphic is drawn,
      in proportion, which is what "full and clearly visible" asks and is a
      different act from altering a mark. It also lands on a happy accident: at
      31x15 CSS pixels a 2x display draws it at 62x30 device pixels, which is
      the file's own resolution.
    */
    width: 31px;
    height: 15px;
    padding: 0.075rem 0.15rem;
    box-sizing: content-box;
    border-radius: 0.15rem;
    background: rgba(242, 232, 220, 0.72);
    /*
      ⚠️ **The first thing here that touches the mark's rendered appearance
      rather than only the surface under it**, and it is the owner's call,
      recorded in ADR-0048.

      What it buys: the badge is a lit chip in a near-black room and Google's
      four saturated letters were the loudest colour on a page whose subject is
      book covers. 0.8 takes the edge off the plate and the logo together — one
      value on one element, so the mark is never faded *relative to* its own
      background, which would read as a doctored logo rather than a quiet one.

      What it costs: "clearly visible" is the clause this is closest to, and it
      is the one about the obligation actually being met, so this is as far as
      it goes. Judged from a render at 1x and 2x, not from the number.
    */
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .pattr-link {
    /*
      44px, and it is the height of the whole row: WCAG 2.5.5's touch target,
      not the graphic, is what sets it — so shrinking the graphic buys width and
      nothing else.
    */
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 0.25rem;
    color: #857361;
    /* The one thing here that is not inert. */
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  .pattr-link:hover,
  .pattr-link:focus-visible {
    color: #c9b8a4;
  }

  .pattr-source {
    gap: 0.35rem;
  }

  /*
    Sized to the text rather than to the file's 16px, so it tracks the row's
    0.75rem.

    ⚠️ **Neutral grey, not the link's warm colour.** GitHub's logo guidelines
    say the Invertocat "should only appear in white, black, or in few cases grey
    or green", and a taupe mark would be a recolour. So the mark takes its own
    `color`, which its `fill="currentColor"` follows: #767676 and #bababa are
    the greys with the same relative luminance as the link's #857361 and hover
    #c9b8a4, so the pair still reads as one link at one weight.
    https://brand.github.com/foundations/logo
  */
  .pattr-icon {
    display: block;
    flex: none;
    width: 1.1em;
    height: 1.1em;
    color: #767676;
  }

  .pattr-source:hover .pattr-icon,
  .pattr-source:focus-visible .pattr-icon {
    color: #bababa;
  }

  .pattr-icon :global(svg) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }
</style>
